# Sleek Audio Player — rules for AI assistants

Read DEVELOPMENT.md before making changes. It is the single source of truth
for conventions; the rules below are the hard constraints you must never skip.

## Generated files — never edit by hand
- assets/js/player.min.js and assets/css/player.min.css are BUILT files.
  After ANY change to assets/js/player.js or assets/css/player.css you MUST run:
    python tools/minify.py
  and commit the regenerated .min files together with the source change.
- languages/*.mo are BUILT files. After ANY change to a .po file run:
    python tools/po2mo.py
  New translatable strings also go into the .pot and the de_DE.po.

## Versioning — four places must always match
1. sleek-audio-player.php header: " * Version: X.Y.Z"
2. sleek-audio-player.php: define('SLEEKAUDIO_VERSION', 'X.Y.Z')
3. readme.txt: "Stable tag: X.Y.Z"
4. readme.md: "**Current Version:** X.Y.Z"
Every user-facing change needs a changelog entry in BOTH readme.md and
readme.txt. A code change without a version bump never reaches cached sites
(SLEEKAUDIO_VERSION is the asset cache-buster).

## Refactoring: a step is not done until it is released
Never change structure and behaviour in the same commit. Extend the tests for
an area BEFORE touching it. Keep the public surface frozen (option names,
sap_playlist post type, meta keys, shortcodes, CSS classes, AJAX actions) -
internal PHP symbols may be renamed, stored data may not. Finish every step
with version bump + changelog + green CI + tag, so the previous state stays
one ZIP away. Details in DEVELOPMENT.md.

## Releases are automated - never build them by hand
To publish a release: git tag vX.Y.Z && git push origin vX.Y.Z
The workflow .github/workflows/release.yml then verifies .min/.mo sync and
tag==plugin version, builds the ZIP, and publishes the GitHub Release with
changelog notes. Do not create GitHub Releases manually and do not attach
hand-built ZIPs.

## Architecture constraints
- Frontend assets load ONLY on pages that render a player
  (register_assets / maybe_enqueue_assets / late enqueue in render_player).
  Never add an unconditional wp_enqueue_* for frontend assets.
- Player markup must never contain <br> or <p> and must never rely on
  whitespace between tags (render_player strips it; page builders run
  wpautop over the output).
- A track meta field exists in FOUR places; change all of them together:
  PHP meta box form, addTrackRow() in assets/js/admin.js,
  save_meta() whitelist, sleekaudio_validate_track() defaults.
  Missing one silently destroys saved data.
- Every AJAX handler needs check_ajax_referer AND current_user_can.
  Every output is escaped, every saved field individually sanitized.
- No user-facing hardcoded strings in player.js — pass them via the
  sapSettings.i18n array (wp_localize_script) with an English fallback.
- PHP must stay compatible with PHP 7.4 / WordPress 5.0.
- player.js stays dependency-free (no jQuery, no frameworks, no CDNs).
- The legacy [simple_player] shortcode alias must keep working.

## CI runs on every push
.github/workflows/ci.yml checks PHP 7.4+8.3 syntax, JS syntax, .min/.mo
sync, ZIP cleanliness, version consistency across all four locations,
WordPress Plugin Check, AND end-to-end browser tests (tests/e2e/, Playwright
against a real WordPress via wp-env).

When you change player behaviour, add or update a test in tests/e2e/.
Selectors and interactions belong in tests/e2e/helpers/player.js, never
directly in a spec file. New *.spec.js files are picked up automatically.
Test content lives in tests/seed.php.

E2E tests do NOT cover real devices, locked screens, audible sound, iOS
Safari or page builders - those stay manual checks.

Flaky tests are repaired at the root cause, NEVER calmed with a longer
waitForTimeout. Wait for a condition (waitForFunction / auto-retrying
expect), not for a duration. Consider that the flake may be a real bug.
Never skip or quarantine a test to get green - fix it or delete it and say
so. The only fixed wait allowed is when asserting something does NOT
happen; there is one in menu.spec.js and it is commented as such.

## Testing before commit (browser behaviour is not automated)
A local test environment EXISTS: https://duesseldorp-test.local/ (LocalWP).
This repo is junction-linked into its plugins folder — every edit is live
there immediately, and SCRIPT_DEBUG serves the unminified sources, so you
can verify JS/CSS changes without rebuilding (still run minify before
committing). Plugin debug output lands in the test site's
wp-content/debug.log. If the site is unreachable, ask the user to start
it in the LocalWP app.

Check at minimum:
1. Page with player via shortcode (early enqueue path)
2. Page-builder page with player (late enqueue + wpautop path)
3. Page WITHOUT player — page source must contain no plugin assets
4. Playback changes: test a full track-to-track transition
Never test against the production site (https://www.duesseldorp.de/).
